Title: Senior/Lead Backend Exercise - Real-time Auction Platform

Goal
You will design and implement a backend system for a high-traffic, real-time
auction platform. The platform must handle large fanout over WebSockets, strict
consistency for bids, and durability in Postgres. Expectation is at least 2 days
of work to design and implement properly.

Tech constraints (must use)
- Python (3.10+ recommended)
- WebSocket-based connections for real-time interactions
- Postgres as the primary database

Optional tech (design only)
- You may propose caches, queues, or stream processors in your design, but the
  implementation must still work with only Python + Postgres.

What we are evaluating (weighted)
- System design (40%)
- Coding skills (40%)
- Low-level design (20%)

Problem statement
Build the backend for a real-time auction system where:
- Each auction has a start/end time, reserve price, and minimum increment.
- Bidders place bids over WebSockets.
- Watchers receive real-time updates (top bid, bid history, auction state).
- The system must enforce ordering, fairness, and atomicity of bids.

Functional requirements
1) Auctions
   - Create an auction with: item_id, start_at, end_at, reserve_price,
     min_increment.
   - State transitions: SCHEDULED -> LIVE -> CLOSED -> SETTLED.
   - Anti-sniping: if a valid bid arrives in the last 30 seconds, extend the
     auction by 30 seconds (repeatable).

2) Bidding
   - Bid placement happens over WebSockets.
   - A bid is valid only if:
     - Auction is LIVE.
     - Bid amount >= current_top + min_increment.
     - Bidder is authenticated and not rate-limited.
   - Bids must be durable and strictly ordered per auction.
   - Reject duplicates via idempotency keys.

3) Real-time updates
   - Clients can subscribe to an auction channel via WebSocket.
   - On subscribe, client receives a snapshot (current top bid + last N bids).
   - After snapshot, client receives live updates.
   - If a client reconnects, it can request updates since last seen sequence.

4) History and audit
   - Store every accepted bid with a monotonic sequence per auction.
   - Provide a way to fetch bid history and final result.

5) Reliability and correctness
   - No lost bids. Once accepted, it must be persisted and replayable.
   - Avoid accepting two winning bids at the same logical time.
   - Handle network disconnects cleanly.

Non-functional requirements (design for these)
- 100k concurrent WebSocket connections across auctions.
- Hot auction: 2k bids/sec, 50k watchers.
- P99 bid ack latency < 200ms.
- 99.95% availability for live auctions.
- Zero data loss for accepted bids.

System design deliverable
Provide a design document that includes:
- Capacity estimates and assumptions.
- Architecture diagram (components and data flow).
- Scaling strategy: horizontal scaling, sharding strategy for auctions.
- WebSocket fanout and backpressure strategy.
- Data model and indexing strategy in Postgres.
- Failure modes and recovery strategies.
- Consistency model for bids and ordering.
- Observability: metrics, logs, tracing, alerts.
- Tradeoffs and alternatives you considered.

Low-level design deliverable
Provide a document or section that includes:
- Module breakdown and key class/function responsibilities.
- Detailed sequence diagrams for:
  - place_bid
  - subscribe_and_replay
  - auction_close_and_settle
- WebSocket message protocol (envelope + message types).
- Database schema with constraints and indexes.
- Concurrency control approach (locks, transactions, isolation levels).
- Idempotency and deduplication strategy.

Coding deliverable (required implementation)
Implement a minimal but correct system that runs locally:
- A WebSocket server that supports:
  - AUTH (token or api_key is fine)
  - SUBSCRIBE {auction_id, since_seq}
  - BID {auction_id, amount, idempotency_key}
- Bid validation and atomic persistence to Postgres.
- Monotonic sequence per auction (no gaps for accepted bids).
- Snapshot + live updates for subscribed clients.
- Anti-sniping behavior.
- Rate limiting per bidder and per auction (simple in-memory acceptable).
- Tests:
  - Concurrent bids for same auction (race condition test).
  - Idempotent bid replay.
  - Anti-sniping extension.

Implementation notes (not requirements)
- Framework choice is yours (FastAPI, Starlette, raw websockets, etc).
- You can keep the implementation as a single service, but show how it would
  scale out.
- Keep dependencies minimal.
- Provide a README with setup steps and how to run tests.

Stretch goals (if time permits)
- Leader election or partition ownership for auctions.
- Backpressure and per-connection flow control.
- Replay from Postgres using a cursor without hot-spotting.
- Fast recovery after node crash (warm caches, rehydrate state).
- TLS, authn/z, and multi-tenant isolation.

Submission format
- README with setup/run/test instructions.
- Design doc (markdown or pdf).
- Code + tests.
- Any scripts or docker compose you want to include.

Clarifications
- You are not expected to implement a full UI.
- You can stub auth, but must show where it integrates.
- You can mock external services in tests.

What good looks like
- Clear, testable design assumptions.
- Correctness under concurrent bidding.
- Practical scaling strategy.
- Clean, readable code with tests and good error handling.
